    Begins iterating over a definite/indefinite text or byte string in chunks. Call it on a string `CborValue` before reading chunks; it asserts if the value isn’t a string or is already in string-iteration mode, then delegates to the internal setup. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_value_begin_string_iteration(CborValue *value);
    ```
4. `cbor_value_get_byte_string_chunk`
    Returns one chunk of a byte string. Use only when `cbor_value_is_byte_string(value)` is true. It fills `bufferptr`/`len` with the current chunk and, if `next` is given, advances to the next chunk/item. It’s a thin wrapper over the generic string-chunk helper. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_value_get_byte_string_chunk(const CborValue *value, const uint8_t **bufferptr,
                                                           size_t *len, CborValue *next);
    ```
5. `cbor_value_get_array_length`
    Gets an array’s definite length. Use only on array values; it asserts if not an array. If length is unknown, returns `CborErrorUnknownLength`. On success writes `*length`; if it won’t fit in `size_t`, returns `CborErrorDataTooLarge`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_value_get_array_length(const CborValue *value, size_t *length);
    ```
6. `cbor_encode_double`
    Encodes a CBOR floating-point value as a double. Use it with a valid `CborEncoder*` and a `double`; it writes the CBOR double representation via `cbor_encode_floating_point`. It returns the `CborError` from that helper. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_encode_double(CborEncoder *encoder, double value);
    ```
7. `cbor_encode_undefined`
    Encodes the CBOR “undefined” simple value. Use it with a valid `CborEncoder*` to write the undefined marker into the output stream. It’s just a thin wrapper over `cbor_encode_simple_value(..., CborUndefinedType & 0x1f)`, so behavior/error handling matches that helper. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_encode_undefined(CborEncoder *encoder);
    ```
8. `cbor_value_dup_text_string`
    `cbor_value_dup_text_string` duplicates a CBOR text string into a newly allocated C string. Use it on a text-string `CborValue`; it fills `*buffer`, optionally `*buflen`, and `*next` with the next item. It asserts if `value` isn’t text. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_value_dup_text_string(const CborValue *value, char **buffer,
                                                     size_t *buflen, CborValue *next);
    ```
9. `cbor_value_is_half_float`
    `cbor_value_is_half_float()` is a simple type check. It returns `true` only when `value->type == CborHalfFloatType`, meaning the current CBOR item is a half-precision float. Use it to test a `CborValue` before reading it as a half float; otherwise it returns `false`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Floating point */
CBOR_INLINE_API bool cbor_value_is_half_float(const CborValue *value);
    ```
10. `cbor_value_get_type`
    Returns the current CBOR item’s type from a `CborValue` iterator. Use it to inspect what kind of value you’re pointing at before reading it. It simply returns `value->type`; no validation or side effects. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborType cbor_value_get_type(const CborValue *value);
    ```
11. `cbor_value_get_map_length`
    Returns a map’s item count when the CBOR map has a definite length. Use only on map values. If length is unknown, it returns `CborErrorUnknownLength`; if the count won’t fit in `size_t`, `CborErrorDataTooLarge`; otherwise `CborNoError`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_value_get_map_length(const CborValue *value, size_t *length);
    ```
12. `cbor_encode_half_float`
    `cbor_encode_half_float` is a thin wrapper to encode a CBOR half-precision float (16-bit). Pass a `CborEncoder*` and a pointer to the float value bytes; it delegates to `cbor_encode_floating_point(..., CborHalfFloatType, ...)` and returns its `CborError` result. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_encode_half_float(CborEncoder *encoder, const void *value);
    ```
13. `cbor_value_get_simple_type`
    Returns the simple-value payload from a `CborValue`. Use only after confirming `cbor_value_is_simple_type(value)`; it asserts otherwise. On success, writes `value->extra` to `*result` and returns `CborNoError`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_value_get_simple_type(const CborValue *value, uint8_t *result);
    ```
14. `cbor_encoder_get_buffer_size`
    Returns how many bytes the encoder has written into `buffer` so far: `encoder->data.ptr - buffer`. Use it after encoding to get the used size. It assumes `buffer` is the original start address; if not, the result is meaningless. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API size_t cbor_encoder_get_buffer_size(const CborEncoder *encoder, const uint8_t *buffer);
    ```
15. `cbor_value_is_text_string`
    Checks whether a `CborValue` currently points to a CBOR text string. Use it on a valid, initialized `CborValue` to test the item type before reading as text. Returns `true` only when `value->type == CborTextStringType`, otherwise `false`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API bool cbor_value_is_text_string(const CborValue *value);
    ```
16. `cbor_encode_null`
    `cbor_encode_null()` writes the CBOR null simple value to the given `CborEncoder`. Use it when you want to encode a CBOR `null` item. It returns the result of `cbor_encode_simple_value()`, so it can fail if the encoder is invalid or out of space. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_encode_null(CborEncoder *encoder);
    ```
17. `cbor_value_is_unsigned_integer`
    `cbor_value_is_unsigned_integer()` is a type-check helper. Use it on a `CborValue*` to test whether the current item is an integer and not marked negative. It returns `true` for non-negative integers, `false` for negative values or non-integers. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API bool cbor_value_is_unsigned_integer(const CborValue *value);
    ```
18. `cbor_value_get_float`
    Gets a CBOR float from a `CborValue` into `*result`. Use only when `cbor_value_is_float(value)` is true. It asserts the value is float and uses the stored 32-bit payload; on success returns `CborNoError`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_value_get_float(const CborValue *value, float *result);
    ```
19. `_cbor_value_get_string_chunk_size`
    Returns the size of the current string chunk and ignores the chunk’s offset. Use it on a `CborValue` pointing at a string chunk; it fills `len` with the chunk length and returns a `CborError`. It’s a thin wrapper over `get_string_chunk_size`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CborError _cbor_value_get_string_chunk_size(const CborValue *value, size_t *len);
    ```
20. `cbor_value_copy_byte_string`
    Copies the current CBOR byte string into `buffer`. Use only when `value` is a byte string; it asserts otherwise. `buflen` is input/output: on entry max space, on return bytes needed/copied. `next` gets the position after the string. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_value_copy_byte_string(const CborValue *value, uint8_t *buffer,
                                                      size_t *buflen, CborValue *next);
    ```
21. `cbor_value_enter_container`
    `cbor_value_enter_container()` initializes a nested iterator for an array/map. Use it on a container `it`, writing the first child state to `recursed`. For indefinite length, it sets `remaining=UINT32_MAX`; for definite length, it reads the length, doubles it for maps, and returns `CborErrorDataTooLarge` on overflow/invalid size. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CborError cbor_value_enter_container(const CborValue *it, CborValue *recursed);
    ```
22. `cbor_value_is_length_known`
    `cbor_value_is_length_known()` checks whether a `CborValue` iterator points to an item with a known length. Use it before relying on `cbor_value_get_array_length`/`_map_length`-style info. It returns `true` when the UnknownLength flag is not set, `false` for indefinite-length items. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API bool cbor_value_is_length_known(const CborValue *value);
    ```
23. `cbor_value_copy_text_string`
    Copies a text string from a `CborValue` into `buffer`, via `_cbor_value_copy_string`. Use only on text-string values (`assert` enforces this). `buflen` is in/out: pass capacity, get bytes copied/needed. `next` receives the following item if requested. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API CborError cbor_value_copy_text_string(const CborValue *value, char *buffer,
                                                      size_t *buflen, CborValue *next);
    ```
24. `cbor_value_is_map`
    `cbor_value_is_map()` is a simple type-check helper. Use it on a `CborValue*` to test whether the current item is a CBOR map. It returns `true` only when `value->type == CborMapType`, otherwise `false`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CBOR_INLINE_API bool cbor_value_is_map(const CborValue *value);
    ```


