Response for query 171 after 8.78s:
This is an assertion-triggered abort, not a memory corruption crash. libFuzzer reports a “deadly signal” because tinycbor calls `assert()` inside `cbor_value_begin_string_iteration`, which then aborts the process.

The crashing library code is:

```c
assert(!(value->flags & CborIteratorFlag_IteratingStringChunks));
```

So the immediate cause is that `cbor_value_begin_string_iteration()` is being called on a `CborValue` that is already marked as “currently iterating string chunks”.

The root cause is in the fuzz driver’s state handling. In `exercise_value`, when the current item is a text/byte string, the driver copies `*it` into `chunk`, starts string-chunk iteration on `chunk`, and later writes `*it = chunk`. That propagates the internal iterator state/flags from the chunk iterator back into the main iterator. On later loop iterations, the driver may see the value as a string again and call `cbor_value_begin_string_iteration(&chunk)` on a `CborValue` whose `flags` already contain `CborIteratorFlag_IteratingStringChunks`. This violates tinycbor’s API invariant and triggers the assertion.

In short: the bug is misuse of the tinycbor iterator API by the fuzz driver, specifically copying back a `CborValue` that still carries internal string-iteration state, then re-entering string iteration on it.
